  <!-- ══════════════════════ WHAT IS A CUT OFF CALCULATOR ══════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-is" class="scroll-mt-20 border-b border-slate-200 bg-white py-14 sm:py-18">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 sm:px-6">
      <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-blue-600">Start here</span>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-2xl">
        What Is a Cut Off Calculator, and Why Does the Answer Change With Every State?
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-[1.0625rem] leading-[1.75] text-slate-700">
        A <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">cut off calculator</strong> is a tool that converts the
        raw marks printed on your Class 12 board marksheet and your entrance scorecard into the single merit
        figure that a state counselling authority ranks on. That figure is rarely your percentage and almost
        never your entrance rank. It is a weighted number, and the weights are published in an admission
        brochure that differs from state to state and sometimes from course to course within the same state.
        A student with 88 in Mathematics, 76 in Physics and 81 in Chemistry produces a 166.5 out of 200 merit
        score in Tamil Nadu, a completely different composite in Karnataka, and a third number again in Kerala
        &mdash; from the identical three marks. This is why a cut off calculator that offers one generic
        formula for all of India is worse than no calculator at all: it produces a confident number that no
        counselling portal will ever accept.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Three numbers that are not the same thing</h3>
          <p class="mt-2.5 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.75] text-slate-700">
            Students routinely confuse these three, and the confusion is where most bad advice online begins.
            Your <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">percentage</strong> is your total divided by the
            maximum, and it tells you almost nothing about merit because it treats every subject as equal. Your
            <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">entrance score</strong> is what one test measured out
            of its own maximum. Your <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">merit or cut off score</strong>
            is the weighted blend of the two that the authority actually sorts the merit list on. A calculator
            gives you the third number, and only the third number is what gets you a seat called.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.75] text-slate-700">
            On top of that sits the <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">closing mark</strong>, the last
            score at which a seat was filled in a previous round. A closing mark is a historical outcome, shaped
            by seat availability and category reservation, not a rule you can apply to yourself. Comparing your
            live merit score against a previous year&rsquo;s closing mark is the single most common arithmetic
            mistake in Indian admission planning, and it is why two calculators on two sites can both be
            correct and still disagree with each other.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">What every calculator here actually does</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2.5 space-y-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.75] text-slate-700">
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-blue-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>
                <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Applies the published weighting exactly.</strong>
                Subject halving, board-plus-exam ratios, percentile cut-offs and entrance scaling are
                transcribed clause by clause from the brochure, then shown as a line-by-line breakdown you can
                audit by hand.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-blue-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>
                <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Separates the merit score from the eligibility gate.</strong>
                Many states also impose a minimum, such as 50% of HSC PCM marks for MHT CET or the 40 marks
                out of 160 that OC and BC candidates need in EAPCET. Missing that gate blocks the seat no matter
                how high the merit score is, so the tool checks it separately.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-blue-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>
                <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Names the tie-breaker that will decide your rank.</strong>
                Where hundreds of candidates finish on an identical composite, the published tie-breaking order
                is what separates them. On most exams the first tie-breaker is Biology, then Chemistry, then
                Physics.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5">
              <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-blue-600" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span>
                <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Never invents a cutoff.</strong> There is no
                predicted cutoff on this site dressed up as an official one. If a number is not in a published
                rule, it is not claimed as one.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="dashed-callout mt-8 max-w-[72ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-[1.75] text-slate-700">
        <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">The one rule that protects you:</strong> always identify
        the conducting authority before you calculate anything. Tamil Nadu engineering is run by DoTE, Karnataka
        by KEA, Kerala by CEE, Gujarat by ACPC, Maharashtra by the State CET Cell, NEET by the NTA under NMC
        rules. Find that name on your scorecard, then use only the calculator published by that body&rsquo;s
        rule. Anything else is arithmetic on the wrong scale.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

